# useUpdateEffect

Runs a side effect when some reactive values change, but not when the component mounts. The library uses it internally, for example to reset the cropper when a new image has loaded.

## Signature

```ts
function useUpdateEffect(effect: () => void | (() => void), deps: () => unknown): void;
```

## Parameters

| Parameter | Description                                                                                                                                    |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `effect`  | The function to run. It may return a cleanup function, which runs before the next run and when the component is destroyed. It runs untracked. |
| `deps`    | A **getter**. Every reactive value it reads becomes a dependency. Return one value or an array, for example `() => [width, height]`.          |

## Return value

None.

## When to call it

During component initialisation, like `$effect`, which it is built on. It is skipped during server-side rendering.

On mount, `deps` is compared with the value it had when the hook was created, and `effect` only runs if they differ. After that, `effect` runs every time one of the dependencies changes.

## Example

Show a short notice when the aspect ratio changes, but not on the first render:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper, useUpdateEffect } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let { src, aspectRatio }: { src: string; aspectRatio: number } = $props();

	let message = $state('');

	useUpdateEffect(
		() => {
			message = `Aspect ratio changed to ${aspectRatio}`;
			const timer = setTimeout(() => (message = ''), 2000);
			return () => clearTimeout(timer);
		},
		() => aspectRatio
	);
</script>

<Cropper {src} stencilProps={{ aspectRatio }} />
{#if message}<p>{message}</p>{/if}
```

## Differences from React

- React takes a dependency array. Svelte takes a getter, and tracks whatever it reads.
- React skips the first run unconditionally. Svelte runs every mount effect in one batch, so a value can change before this effect's first run. To still report that change, the first run compares against the value seen at creation instead of skipping. Values are compared deeply.
- In everyday Svelte code you can usually write an `$effect` instead. The function is exported for parity with react-advanced-cropper and for code ported from it.
